
  [1mThe fork:[0m what makes a task one you "care about." This is the single decision that determines whether the prototype
  feels smart or noisy, and it shapes the data model — each option stores something different.

  - [1mAssigned to you[0m — you're notified about your own work. Cheap, obvious, needs only an [38;5;153massignee[39m field. But it can't
    express "I'm not doing this, but I need to know when it ships," which is often the interesting case.
  - [1mExplicitly watched[0m — you star/follow tasks. Needs a watchers list per task. Buys precision and makes the concept
    visible in the UI (a follow button is a thing you can [3mdemo[0m). Costs: an empty watch list means an empty feed, so the
    prototype needs seeding to look alive.
  - [1mImplicit involvement[0m — you're auto-subscribed by touching a task (created it, commented, were assigned). Feels
    magical when it works; it's how GitHub behaves. Costs: needs an activity/participation record, and it's the hardest
    to make legible in a demo because the rules are invisible.
  - [1mEverything[0m — a global activity feed, no subscription concept. Trivial to build, and honestly a decent prototype
    baseline. But it sidesteps the "tasks they care about" part entirely, which is the interesting half of your request.

  [1mMy recommendation: explicitly watched, with assignment auto-adding you as a watcher.[0m It's one concept (a watcher list)
  that covers both common cases, it's directly visible on screen — a follow button and a badge count are things you can
  point at in a demo — and it's the cheapest of the meaningful options to swap out later, since the feed reads from one
  list regardless of how names got into it.
